          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Borel-Weil theorem
            characterizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            of suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+line+bundles"} complex line bundles
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+varieties"} flag varieties
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+subgroup"} Borel subgroup
            %em Borel-Weil-Bott theorem
            generalizes this to higher cohomologies.
          %p
            With suitable qualifiers added this is Kirillov’s
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            equipped with
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            to its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} fiber integration
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            and accordingly the Borel-Weil-Bott theorem is naturally regarded in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Schubert+calculus"} generalized Schubert calculus
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Kumar12"} Kumar 12
          %p
            A common method of construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            is to consider the invariant subspaces of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induced+representation"} induced representation
            (set-theoretic or
            = succeed "-version)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+reciprocity"} Frobenius reciprocity
            indicates that they are normally not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\subset G
            and a
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\colon B\to Aut(V)
            the induced module can be represented as a space of (set-theoretic or
            = succeed "\u2011)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi B
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times_{G/B} V
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+fiber+bundle"} principal fiber bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\to G/B
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"} connection
            on the bundle.
          %p
            The first instance is the theorem of Borel–Weil, (J-P. Serre, Bourbaki Seminar 100, 1953/54) which asserts that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+subgroup"} Borel subgroup
            of the complex semisimple group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\mathbb{C}}
            (which can be considered as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+torus"} maximal torus
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo =
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T=G\cap B\subset G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\to G/B
            over the generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+variety"} flag variety
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\mathbb{C}}/B\cong G/T
            with the fiber
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}_\chi
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/character"} character
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
          %p
            There are numerous extensions e.g. to Harish–Chandra sheaves to construct the infinite-dimensional representations. The original proof is by geometric and analytic methods; some of the modern extensions of the method use the algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-module"} D-module
            theory and are based on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Bernstein+localization"} Beilinson-Bernstein localization
            theorem. There are even extensions to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+groups"} quantum groups
